  "entries": {
    "hop": {
      "word": "HOP",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To move by successive leaps, as toads do; to spring or jump on one foot; to skip, as birds do. [Birds] hopping from spray to spray. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To walk lame; to limp; to halt. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To dance. Smollett.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A leap on one leg, as of a boy; a leap, as of a toad; a jump; a spring.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A dance; esp., an informal dance of ball. [Colloq.] Hop, skip (or step), and jump, a game or athletic sport in which the participants cover as much ground as possible by a hop, stride, and jump in succession. Addison.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Bot.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A climbing plant (Humulus Lupulus), having a long, twining, annual stalk. It is cultivated for its fruit (hops).",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The catkin or strobilaceous fruit of the hop, much used in brewing to give a bitter taste.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The fruit of the dog-rose. See Hip. Hop back. (Brewing) See under 1st Back. hops in miniature (Trifolium agrarium, and T. procumbens). injurious to hops. hop vines. interrupta), allied to the cockoo spits. It often does great damage to hop vines. (O.Virginica) the American ironwood; also, a European species (O. vulgaris). state is very injurious to hop vines. having broad, flattened fruit in large clusters, sometimes used as a substitute for hops.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To impregnate with hops. Mortimer.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To gather hops. [Perhaps only in the form Hopping, vb. n.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
